{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794685404,"nonce":"yftZZxewvAuakjlrHNupuzdLfBo=","message":"Rayzor as the macro host, 2026-11-14. Idea: run the exporter macro in rayzor's macro interpreter and ship the rayzor CLI if it is faster.\n\nResult: it is not faster, and the exporter cannot run there as it stands.\n\n- Speed, same workload as the exporter's inner loop (300k-node tree, BFS walk writing 7 Ints per node, Map interning, enum switch), quiet NUC, three reps, matching checksums: Haxe 4.3.6 eval 655 to 674 ms; rayzor interpreter 7.6 to 8.0 s; rayzor with RAYZOR_MACRO_VM=1 7.6 to 7.9 s. About 11.5 times slower, and the VM tier made no difference here.\n- API: rayzor's `onGenerate`/`onAfterTyping` hooks receive `ModuleType` values built from parsed declarations (name, package, module, metadata). There are no typed fields or bodies, and `Type.getInstanceFields`/`getClass` answer only for a few built-in types. The exporter needs every field's typed body.\n- Correctness: macro Ints do not wrap at 32 bits (`max+1` is 2147483648 in a macro, -2147483648 in rayzor's compiled code and in Haxe eval). Filed in rayzor as 7d1947c; the speed finding as 2cd3b36.\n- Measurement trap: `rayzor run` replays cached macro results; use `--no-cache`.\n\nNot tested: a native exporter inside rayzor over its own Rust typed AST (no interpreter involved). Whether rayzor's typer is faithful enough to Haxe 4.3.6, and whether it types the HL std, is a separate question.","files":null}]}